 {
  "id": "wa_hozomeen_mountain_west_face",
  "name": "West Face (North Peak)",
  "peakName": "Hozomeen Mountain",
  "state": "Washington",
  "discipline": "alpine",
  "status": "stuck",
  "peak": {
   "id": "wa_hozomeen_mountain",
   "name": "Hozomeen Mountain",
   "lat": 48.982185,
   "lng": -121.012035,
   "elevation_ft": 8071,
   "region": "Ross Lake/Jack Mountain",
   "path": "usa.washington.wa_northwest.wa_hwy20_ncnp.wa_hozomeen_mountain"
  },
  "text": {
   "approach": "A two-stage approach and the second stage is the part that stops people. Day one is a long haul of 11-plus miles under heavy loads from the road to a camp just north of the peak - the same Hozomeen Ridge / border-swath ground used by the north-side scrambling routes, reached from Silver-Skagit Road out of Hope, B.C. From that camp the approach to the base took the first ascent party a tedious and painstaking 4.5 hours: downclimb a loose third-class gully, which the party described as dangerous but the lesser of the two evils, then cross several precipitous rocky ribs and steep, unstable ground to traverse into the launch point below the wall. Budget the base approach as a half-day objective in its own right and do it in daylight. Confirm the NPS border-gate status before planning; the crossing at Hozomeen has been closed to vehicles and foot traffic since 2020, with no end date given on the park's fire-closure page updated 14 September 2026.",
   "approach_logistics": null,
   "road": "{\"name\":\"Silver-Skagit Road (Skagit Valley Road) from Hope, British Columbia\",\"status\":\"About 37 miles / 60 km of gravel, graded periodically but usually washboarded and potholed; carry a spare tire. Rebuilt after the 2021 B.C. flood washouts. Closed at km 6 as of 24 July 2026 for the Border 2 Fire, which crossed into B.C. 2.4 km east of Hozomeen.\",\"driveNote\":\"Hwy 1 Exit 168 at Hope, Flood-Hope Road, then Silver-Skagit Road south. 1 to 1.5 hours. Skyline II trailhead near km 55 serves the northern approach to a camp north of the peak.\",\"seasonalGate\":\"B.C. does not maintain Silver-Skagit Road",
   "overview": "A Grade IV, 5.9 technical rock route on the North Peak's west face, very difficult to protect and among the hardest routes on the mountain.",
   "description": null,
   "descent": "From the summit, take the easy north-side ground: reverse the Northeast Buttress / North Route line, downclimbing the Class 2-3 northeast ridge, treating the short very exposed Class 4 eroded segment near 7,600 ft with maximum care, then descending the north cirque snowfields to the saddle and back to camp. In late summer, when this route is in condition, those snowfields will be thin or gone, lea"
  },
